# WHO OWNS THE EDGE OF EACH RANGE. One row per answer, with how many claims gave it.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE PAIR WITH narrowsWhen IS THE POINT: one says what would make a range smaller, the
#    other says who owns the edge it would have to move. A negotiated bound moves by
#    renegotiating; a bound that is simply where the measurements fell moves by measuring
#    again; an intrinsic one does not move at all. Same width, three different courses of
#    action, and only one of them is a purchase order.
#
# ⛔ AS AN OPTIONAL BARE ENUMERATION IT WAS FILED ONCE IN 124 CLAIMS. Required and typed, the
#   answer that leads is that NOTHING sets the edge -- the range is simply where the
#   measurements fell -- with `derived` close behind it, where the model ALREADY states the
#   author in a sibling element and had no way to point at it. Neither is a finding the single
#   column could have produced.
#   ⚠️ `derived` LED WHEN THIS WAS WRITTEN AND THE COMMENT SAID SO AS THOUGH IT WERE SETTLED.
#     The corpus grew and `none` passed it, with the table one line below still printing the
#     refutation. A count in a comment is a claim with a date on it; prefer the shape of the
#     answer to its rank, or read the number off the run.
-- pm:Claim/pm:boundOrigin, over the corpus.
SELECT e.who_owns_the_edge, count(*) AS claims
FROM (
    :compose(epistemics/edges.sqlc)
) e
JOIN (
    :compose(scope/corpus.sqlc)
) s USING (filing)
GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 2 DESC
